+## Decisions & Alternatives
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrent-edit policy | Whole-visit revisions: competing complete revisions, coordinator selects | Field-aware three-way merge; last-write-wins overwrite | Maintained merge rules can combine scientifically related edits — e.g., one worker's identification fix with another worker's cover estimate taken under the old identification — without anyone noticing. The team accepts a larger review volume as the cost of never auto-combining. Last-write-wins silently discards a submitted edit. |
+| Submission arriving during an open review | Joins the open review as a competing revision | Fast-forward it when its base equals current; reject or hold it | Fixes the comparison set: coordinators never review against a moving current record, and a review outcome can never displace an edit that was accepted onto the chain. |
+| Resolution shape | Selects one submitted revision (or keeps current); hybrid content via a follow-up submission | Coordinator composes a merged record at resolution | Every current revision stays identical to a submitted payload, keeping provenance one line. Composition would re-import field-level merge judgment into the review step. |
+| Stale review protection | Resolution carries the reviewed competing set; mismatch rejects it | Last-wins resolution | Two coordinators may work the same queue; rejecting a stale view prevents closing a review past a competing revision nobody saw. |
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+### Resolved
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+1. ✅ Concurrent-edit policy: whole-visit revisions (Decisions & Alternatives, row 1).
+2. ✅ Submission envelope, visit states, and review operations are defined; tablet-sync and review-export build on them.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+### Deferred
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+1. Duplicate independent visit creation (two workers creating visits for the same plot and date under different UUIDs) — not a concurrency-contract matter; revisit as a review-export data-quality concern.
+2. Transport and error payload details — settled at implementation; this design fixes semantics, not endpoint shapes.
+3. Retention horizon — submissions and outcomes are retained indefinitely at ~6,000 visits per season; revisit only if storage becomes a concern.
+4. Whether new visits should be restricted to active plots — validation accepts any catalog-resolvable plot UUID; the new-visit picker already excludes archived plots (site-catalog).
 
